terior, it outputs color 2. I wonder if this is due to a pattern matching rule. I’ll apply the fill of 7 to all holes and test various frames—like 3x3 inside a large box and others—to ensure consistency. I'll focus on identifying the borders and filling indeed enclosed components.**Exploring flood fill techniques**\n\nI'm considering the flood fill method for replacing non-background enclosed cells. The idea would be to connect to edges as a guideline. For the boundaries of objects, I believe that all interior cells should be treated as background. I’ll need to account for multiple shapes in this process too. My goal is to ensure that every enclosed space is filled properly while maintaining the object boundaries clearly. This should help streamline the overall task!","t":1792729498.364382,"summary":true},{"type":"tool","name":"ipython","input":"os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p),exist_ok=True)\ncode='''from collections import deque\\ndef transform(grid):\\n    h,w=len(grid),len(grid[0]); bg=grid[0][0]\\n    seen=set(); q=deque()\\n    for r in range(h):\\n        for c in range(w):\\n            if r in (0,h-1) or c in (0,w-1):\\n                if grid[r][c]==bg and (r,c) not in seen: seen.add((r,c)); q.append((r,c))\\n    while q:\\n        r,c=q.popleft()\\n        for dr,dc in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):\\n            nr,nc=r+dr,c+dc\\n            if 0<=nr<h and 0<=nc<w and grid[nr][nc]==bg and (nr,nc) not in seen:\\n                seen.add((nr,nc)); q.append((nr,nc))\\n    out=[row[:] for row in grid]\\n    for r in range(h):\\n        for c in range(w):\\n            if grid[r][c]==bg and (r,c) not in seen: out[r][c]=7\\n    return out\\n'''\nopen(p,'w').write(code)\ngrid=[[6,9,9,9,9,9,9,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6],[6,9,6,6,6,6,9,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6],[6,9,6,6,6,6,9,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6],[6,9,6,6,6,6,9,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6],[6,9,6,6,6,6,9,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6],[6,9,9,9,9,9,9,6,6,0,0,0,0,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6],[6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,